\section{Keys and row order}{


In \code{collapse_dims()}, the dimensions that are kept
are the \code{by} variables or, if \code{data} is a
\href{https://dplyr.tidyverse.org/reference/group_data.html}{grouped}
data frame, the grouping variables. Measures are
removed from \code{by} automatically, so \code{by = !region}
keeps every dimension except \code{region}. All other
dimensions are collapsed, and a message lists them.

In \code{collapse_levels()}, every column that is not a
measure is kept.

Rows appear in the order in which their combination
of dimensions first appears in \code{data}. In \code{collapse_levels()},
if a recoded \code{dim} variable is a factor, then rows are
ordered by its levels, within the remaining dimensions.

The result is an ungrouped \link[tibble:tibble]{tibble}.
}

\section{Specifying the map}{


Argument \code{map} in \code{collapse_levels()} can be
\itemize{
\item \strong{A named list.} Each element gives the old levels that
are merged into the new level given by its name, as in
\code{list(North = c("Northland", "Auckland"))}.
Every old level must appear in \code{data}.
\item \strong{A data frame.} A lookup table with two columns:
old levels first and new levels second. Each old
level appears at most once. The lookup table can
include levels that do not appear in \code{data}.
\item \strong{A function.} A function, such as
\code{\link[=combine_age]{combine_age()}}, that takes a vector of levels and
returns a vector of new levels with the same length,
for instance \verb{\\(x) combine_age(x, to = "lt")}.
The function is applied once to each \code{dim} variable.
Each old level must map to a single new level.
}

With a list or a data frame, levels that
are not mentioned are left unchanged.
If a \code{dim} variable is a factor, or a function or
lookup table returns a factor, then the result is
a factor, and keeps any unused levels.

If \code{dim} specifies more than one variable, such as
origin and destination, the same map is applied to each.
When merged regions are both origins and destinations,
flows between them become flows within the merged region.
}

\section{Using rvecs to represent uncertainty}{


An \link[rvec:rvec]{rvec} is a 'random vector',
holding multiple draws from a distribution.
Measures, including \code{wt}, can be rvecs.
Calculations are done separately for each draw.
A rate, probability, mean, or ratio is an rvec
in the result if it, or \code{wt}, is an rvec.
Dimensions cannot be rvecs.
}

\section{Checks}{

\itemize{
\item When \code{wt_shared} is supplied, \code{wt} must be
constant across the shared dimensions.
\item After values have been summed over shared dimensions,
probabilities must not exceed 1.
\item When \code{rate}, \code{prob}, \code{mean}, \code{ratio}, or \code{wt} is supplied,
\code{data} must not have two rows with the same dimensions.
\item If \code{wt} is identical across the levels of a collapsed
dimension in every case, \code{wt} may be a shared weight,
and the functions give a warning.
\item \code{collapse_levels()} gives a message if no rows are merged.
}

If a weighted mean has total weight of zero,
the result is \code{NA}.
